## Learning objectives
- Design classes with constructors, encapsulated fields and access modifiers.
- Apply the this, static and final keywords correctly.
- Use inheritance, polymorphism, abstract classes, interfaces and packages.

## Why object-oriented programming
Object-oriented programming (OOP) organises code around **objects** that bundle data (fields) with the behaviour (methods) that keeps that data valid. Instead of passing loose variables between functions, you model the domain - `Employee`, `Course`, `Order` - and let each object protect its own rules. The four pillars are **encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism and abstraction**.

## Classes, objects and constructors
### Classes and objects
A **class** is a blueprint; an **object** is an instance created from it with `new`.

```java
public class Course {
    String title;
    int durationHours;

    void describe() {
        System.out.println(title + " (" + durationHours + "h)");
    }
}

Course java = new Course();
java.title = "Java Basics";
java.durationHours = 12;
java.describe();   // Java Basics (12h)
```

Each object has its own copy of instance fields.

### Constructors
A constructor runs when an object is created. It has the class name and no return type. Use it to guarantee a valid starting state.

```java
public class Course {
    private final String title;
    private int durationHours;

    public Course(String title, int durationHours) {
        if (title == null || title.isBlank()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Title required");
        this.title = title;
        this.durationHours = durationHours;
    }

    public Course(String title) {      // overloaded constructor
        this(title, 1);                // calls the other constructor
    }
}
```

If you write no constructor, Java supplies a no-argument default. Once you write any constructor, the default disappears.

## The this, static and final keywords
- **`this`** refers to the current object. Use it to tell a field from a parameter with the same name (`this.title = title`) or to call another constructor (`this(...)`).
- **`static`** members belong to the class, not to any object. `Math.max` and a shared counter are static. A static method cannot use `this` or instance fields directly.
- **`final`** means "cannot change": a final variable is assigned once, a final method cannot be overridden, a final class cannot be extended (`String` is final).

```java
public class Employee {
    private static int created = 0;        // shared by all employees
    private final int id;                  // fixed after construction
    private String name;

    public Employee(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        this.id = ++created;
    }
    public static int count() { return created; }
}

new Employee("Asha");
new Employee("Ravi");
System.out.println(Employee.count());   // 2
```

Do not store per-user data (such as a shopping cart) in a static field - every user would share it.

## Encapsulation and access modifiers
**Encapsulation** hides internal state and exposes controlled operations. Make fields `private` and provide methods that enforce the rules.

```java
public class BankAccount {
    private double balance;

    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount must be positive");
        balance += amount;
    }
    public void withdraw(double amount) {
        if (amount > balance) throw new IllegalStateException("Insufficient funds");
        balance -= amount;
    }
    public double getBalance() { return balance; }
}
```

Nobody can set `balance = -500` from outside; every change goes through validation.

### Access modifiers

| Modifier | Same class | Same package | Subclass (other package) | Everywhere |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `private` | yes | no | no | no |
| (default / package-private) | yes | yes | no | no |
| `protected` | yes | yes | yes | no |
| `public` | yes | yes | yes | yes |

Start with the most restrictive modifier and open up only when needed.

## Inheritance and polymorphism
### Inheritance
A subclass **extends** a parent class and inherits its non-private members. Use inheritance for a true "is-a" relationship.

```java
class Employee {
    protected String name;
    Employee(String name) { this.name = name; }
    double monthlyPay() { return 30000; }
}

class Manager extends Employee {
    private double bonus;
    Manager(String name, double bonus) {
        super(name);                 // call the parent constructor first
        this.bonus = bonus;
    }
    @Override
    double monthlyPay() { return super.monthlyPay() + bonus; }
}
```

Java supports single class inheritance (one parent class) but a class can implement many interfaces. Every class ultimately extends `Object`, which provides `toString`, `equals` and `hashCode`.

### Polymorphism
"Many forms": one reference type, different runtime behaviour.
- **Compile-time polymorphism** - method overloading (same name, different parameters).
- **Runtime polymorphism** - method overriding; the JVM picks the method of the actual object.

```java
List<Employee> staff = List.of(new Employee("Asha"), new Manager("Ravi", 10000));
for (Employee e : staff) {
    System.out.println(e.name + ": " + e.monthlyPay());
}
// Asha: 30000.0
// Ravi: 40000.0
```

The loop is written once against `Employee`, yet each object answers with its own version of `monthlyPay`.

## Abstraction: abstract classes and interfaces
**Abstraction** exposes *what* an object does and hides *how*.

### Abstract classes
An abstract class cannot be instantiated. It can hold state, constructors and concrete methods, plus abstract methods that subclasses must implement.

```java
abstract class Shape {
    abstract double area();
    void print() { System.out.printf("Area: %.2f%n", area()); }
}
class Circle extends Shape {
    private final double r;
    Circle(double r) { this.r = r; }
    double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; }
}
new Circle(2).print();   // Area: 12.57
```

### Interfaces
An interface is a contract: a set of method signatures. A class can implement many interfaces. Since Java 8 interfaces may also have `default` and `static` methods.

```java
interface Notifier {
    void send(String to, String message);
}
class EmailNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String to, String message) {
        System.out.println("Email to " + to + ": " + message);
    }
}
class SmsNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String to, String message) {
        System.out.println("SMS to " + to + ": " + message);
    }
}
```

Code that depends on `Notifier` works with either implementation - this is the basis of dependency injection in Spring.

| Abstract class | Interface |
| --- | --- |
| Can have fields with state and constructors | Only constants (no instance state) |
| A class extends only one | A class implements many |
| Use for closely related classes sharing code | Use for a capability shared by unrelated classes |

## Packages
A **package** groups related classes and prevents name clashes. The package matches the folder structure.

```java
// file: src/com/shimento/lms/model/Course.java
package com.shimento.lms.model;

public class Course { /* ... */ }

// another file
import com.shimento.lms.model.Course;
```

Convention: reverse domain name, all lowercase (`com.company.project.layer`).

## Common mistakes
- Public fields that let callers bypass validation.
- Using inheritance for code reuse when the relationship is "has-a" - prefer composition (a field of the other type).
- Forgetting `super(...)` when the parent has no no-argument constructor.
- Overloading when you meant overriding (a different parameter type creates a new method). Always add `@Override`.
- Static mutable state shared accidentally between users or threads.

## Practice exercise
1. Create an abstract class `Employee` with `name`, `id` and an abstract `double monthlyPay()`.
2. Add `FullTimeEmployee` (fixed salary) and `Contractor` (hourly rate × hours).
3. Create an interface `Taxable` with `double tax()` and implement it in both classes.
4. Put the classes in a package `com.yourname.payroll` and print a payroll report for a mixed `List<Employee>`.
5. Make all fields private, validate constructor arguments and use `final` where a value never changes.

## Key takeaways
- A class is a blueprint, an object is an instance; constructors establish a valid state.
- `this` refers to the current object, `static` belongs to the class, `final` prevents change.
- Encapsulation = private fields + validating methods.
- Inheritance models "is-a"; polymorphism lets one call site run different implementations.
- Abstract classes share code among related types; interfaces define contracts for any type.

## Learning objectives
- Distinguish checked exceptions, unchecked exceptions and errors.
- Handle failures with try-catch, finally and try-with-resources.
- Use throw, throws and custom exceptions appropriately.

## Introduction to exceptions
An **exception** is an event that disrupts the normal flow of a program: a missing file, invalid input, a network timeout or a bug such as dividing by zero. Java represents these events as objects. When something goes wrong, the code **throws** an exception, and the JVM unwinds the call stack until it finds code that **catches** it. If nobody catches it, the thread stops and the stack trace is printed.

### The exception hierarchy

```
Throwable
├── Error                  (serious JVM problems: OutOfMemoryError, StackOverflowError)
└── Exception
    ├── IOException, SQLException ...        (checked)
    └── RuntimeException                       (unchecked)
        ├── NullPointerException
        ├── ArithmeticException
        ├── IllegalArgumentException
        └── ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
```

| Type | Must you handle or declare it? | Typical cause | Examples |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Checked | Yes - the compiler enforces it | External conditions the program should anticipate | `IOException`, `SQLException` |
| Unchecked (`RuntimeException`) | No | Programming errors or broken method contracts | `NullPointerException`, `IllegalArgumentException` |
| `Error` | No - do not catch normally | JVM/resource failure | `OutOfMemoryError` |

## The try-catch block
Put code that may fail inside `try`, and handle specific exceptions in `catch` blocks.

```java
public class SafeDivide {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            int total = Integer.parseInt(args[0]);
            int parts = Integer.parseInt(args[1]);
            System.out.println("Each part: " + total / parts);
        } catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
            System.out.println("Usage: java SafeDivide <total> <parts>");
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.out.println("Please enter whole numbers: " + e.getMessage());
        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
            System.out.println("Parts cannot be zero");
        }
        System.out.println("Program continues");
    }
}
```

`java SafeDivide 10 0` prints:

```
Parts cannot be zero
Program continues
```

Rules:
- Order catch blocks from **most specific to most general**; a `catch (Exception e)` before `catch (NumberFormatException e)` will not compile.
- Use **multi-catch** when the handling is identical: `catch (NumberFormatException | ArithmeticException e)`.
- Keep the `try` block small so you know exactly which operation failed.

## final, finally and finalize
These three look alike but are unrelated.

| Keyword | What it is | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `final` | Modifier: variable cannot be reassigned, method cannot be overridden, class cannot be extended | `final int MAX = 3;` |
| `finally` | Block that runs after `try`/`catch` whether or not an exception occurred | closing a resource, releasing a lock |
| `finalize()` | Old `Object` method the GC might call before reclaiming an object - deprecated, unreliable, do not use | - |

```java
Scanner in = null;
try {
    in = new Scanner(new File("data.txt"));
    System.out.println(in.nextLine());
} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
    System.out.println("File missing");
} finally {
    if (in != null) in.close();   // always runs
    System.out.println("Cleanup done");
}
```

### try-with-resources (preferred)
Any object implementing `AutoCloseable` can be declared in the `try` header; Java closes it automatically, even when an exception is thrown.

```java
try (Scanner in = new Scanner(new File("data.txt"))) {
    System.out.println(in.nextLine());
} catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
    System.out.println("File missing");
}
```

This is shorter and safer than a manual `finally`. Use it for files, streams, JDBC connections and statements.

## throw and throws
- **`throw`** actually raises an exception object: `throw new IllegalArgumentException("Age must be positive");`
- **`throws`** in a method signature declares that the method may pass a checked exception to its caller.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;

public class ConfigReader {
    // declares the checked exception; the caller decides how to handle it
    static String readFirstLine(String path) throws IOException {
        return Files.readAllLines(Path.of(path)).get(0);
    }

    static void setAge(int age) {
        if (age < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Age cannot be negative: " + age);
        System.out.println("Age set to " + age);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            System.out.println(readFirstLine("app.conf"));
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.out.println("Could not read config: " + e.getMessage());
        }
        setAge(30);    // Age set to 30
        setAge(-1);    // throws IllegalArgumentException, program stops here
    }
}
```

## Custom exceptions
Create your own exception class when callers need to recognise a specific business failure.

```java
public class InsufficientBalanceException extends Exception {
    public InsufficientBalanceException(double needed) {
        super("Insufficient balance, need " + needed + " more");
    }
}

class Account {
    private double balance = 100;
    void withdraw(double amount) throws InsufficientBalanceException {
        if (amount > balance) throw new InsufficientBalanceException(amount - balance);
        balance -= amount;
    }
}
```

Extend `Exception` for a checked exception or `RuntimeException` for an unchecked one. Spring applications usually use unchecked business exceptions and map them to HTTP responses (Module 28).

## Best practices
- Catch only what you can handle meaningfully; otherwise let it propagate.
- Never swallow exceptions with an empty `catch {}` - at minimum log them.
- Preserve the cause when wrapping: `throw new ServiceException("Import failed", e);`
- Do not use exceptions for normal control flow (for example, to end a loop).
- Do not show stack traces or secrets to end users; log details, show a friendly message.
- Release resources with try-with-resources.

## Common mistakes
- `catch (Exception e) {}` hiding real bugs.
- Catching a general exception before a specific one (compile error).
- Thinking `finally` is skipped when `return` appears in `try` - it still runs.
- Confusing `throw` (an action) with `throws` (a declaration).

## Practice exercise
1. Write a method `int parseAge(String text)` that throws `IllegalArgumentException` for non-numeric text or ages outside 0-120.
2. Create a checked `InvalidMarksException` and throw it from `double percentage(int obtained, int total)` when `total <= 0` or `obtained > total`.
3. Read numbers from `marks.txt` using try-with-resources; for each bad line, print the line number and continue.
4. Print a summary: lines read, valid lines, invalid lines.

## Key takeaways
- Checked exceptions must be caught or declared; unchecked exceptions signal programming errors.
- Order catch blocks from specific to general; keep `try` blocks small.
- `finally` always runs; try-with-resources is the modern way to close resources.
- `throw` raises an exception; `throws` declares one.
- Custom exceptions make business failures explicit.

## Learning objectives
- Explain how generics provide compile-time type safety.
- Write generic classes, interfaces and methods with bounded type parameters.
- Use wildcards correctly and explain the consequences of type erasure.

## Introduction to generics
Before Java 5, collections stored plain `Object` references. You had to cast every element and a wrong type was discovered only at runtime:

```java
List list = new ArrayList();      // raw type - avoid
list.add("hello");
list.add(42);                     // compiles!
String s = (String) list.get(1);  // ClassCastException at runtime
```

**Generics** let you parameterise a type with other types, so the compiler checks them for you:

```java
List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
names.add("hello");
// names.add(42);                 // compile error - caught early
String s = names.get(0);          // no cast needed
```

Benefits: **compile-time type safety, no casts, and reusable, self-documenting code**. The `<>` on the right side is the *diamond operator*; the compiler infers the type argument.

## Generic classes
Declare one or more **type parameters** in angle brackets after the class name. By convention they are single capital letters: `T` (type), `E` (element), `K`/`V` (key/value), `R` (result).

```java
public class Box<T> {
    private T value;

    public Box(T value) { this.value = value; }
    public T get() { return value; }
    public boolean isEmpty() { return value == null; }
}

Box<String> nameBox = new Box<>("Asha");
Box<Integer> ageBox = new Box<>(30);
String name = nameBox.get();          // no cast
int age = ageBox.get();               // auto-unboxed
```

A class can have several parameters:

```java
public record Pair<K, V>(K key, V value) { }

Pair<String, Double> price = new Pair<>("laptop", 55000.0);
System.out.println(price.key() + " = " + price.value());   // laptop = 55000.0
```

Type arguments must be reference types: write `Box<Integer>`, not `Box<int>`.

### Generic methods
A method can declare its own type parameter, placed before the return type, even inside a non-generic class.

```java
public class Utils {
    public static <T> T firstOrDefault(List<T> list, T fallback) {
        return list.isEmpty() ? fallback : list.get(0);
    }
}

String first = Utils.firstOrDefault(List.of("x", "y"), "none");   // "x"
Integer n = Utils.firstOrDefault(List.of(), 0);                   // 0
```

## Generic interfaces
Interfaces can be generic too. `Comparable<T>`, `List<E>` and `Map<K,V>` are all generic interfaces. A typical full-stack example is a repository contract:

```java
public interface Repository<T, ID> {
    void save(T entity);
    Optional<T> findById(ID id);
    List<T> findAll();
}

public class InMemoryCourseRepository implements Repository<Course, Long> {
    private final Map<Long, Course> store = new HashMap<>();
    public void save(Course c) { store.put(c.id(), c); }
    public Optional<Course> findById(Long id) { return Optional.ofNullable(store.get(id)); }
    public List<Course> findAll() { return new ArrayList<>(store.values()); }
}
```

You will meet exactly this pattern in Spring Data's `JpaRepository<Entity, ID>` (Module 29).

An implementing class can either fix the type (`implements Repository<Course, Long>`) or stay generic (`class MemoryRepo<T, ID> implements Repository<T, ID>`).

## Bounded type parameters
Sometimes the type must have certain capabilities. An **upper bound** restricts `T` to a type and its subtypes with `extends` (used for both classes and interfaces here).

```java
public static <T extends Number> double sum(List<T> values) {
    double total = 0;
    for (T v : values) total += v.doubleValue();   // allowed: T is a Number
    return total;
}

sum(List.of(1, 2, 3));          // 6.0
sum(List.of(1.5, 2.5));         // 4.0
// sum(List.of("a"));           // compile error
```

Multiple bounds use `&`, with the class first: `<T extends Number & Comparable<T>>`.

```java
public static <T extends Comparable<T>> T max(List<T> list) {
    T best = list.get(0);
    for (T item : list) if (item.compareTo(best) > 0) best = item;
    return best;
}
max(List.of(3, 9, 4));               // 9
max(List.of("pear", "apple"));       // pear
```

## Wildcards
The wildcard `?` means "some unknown type". It is used in parameters to make methods flexible.

Important: `List<Integer>` is **not** a subtype of `List<Number>`, even though `Integer` is a subtype of `Number`. Wildcards solve this.

| Wildcard | Meaning | You can | Example use |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `<?>` | Any type | Read as `Object` | `printAll(List<?> list)` |
| `<? extends T>` | T or a subtype (upper bound) | Read as T; cannot add (except `null`) | A **producer** you read from |
| `<? super T>` | T or a supertype (lower bound) | Add T; read only as `Object` | A **consumer** you write to |

```java
static double total(List<? extends Number> nums) {   // accepts List<Integer>, List<Double>
    double t = 0;
    for (Number n : nums) t += n.doubleValue();
    return t;
}

static void addDefaults(List<? super Integer> target) { // accepts List<Integer>, List<Number>, List<Object>
    target.add(0);
    target.add(1);
}
```

Remember **PECS: Producer Extends, Consumer Super**. `Collections.copy(List<? super T> dest, List<? extends T> src)` follows exactly this rule.

## Type erasure
Generics exist only at compile time. The compiler checks types, inserts the necessary casts, then **erases** type parameters from the bytecode - `List<String>` and `List<Integer>` both become plain `List` at runtime (an unbounded `T` becomes `Object`; `T extends Number` becomes `Number`).

Consequences of erasure:
- You cannot write `new T()` or `new T[10]`.
- You cannot use `instanceof List<String>` (use `instanceof List<?>`).
- You cannot overload methods that differ only in type arguments: `void f(List<String>)` and `void f(List<Integer>)` clash.
- Static fields cannot use the class's type parameter.
- Primitive type arguments are not allowed.

```java
List<String> a = new ArrayList<>();
List<Integer> b = new ArrayList<>();
System.out.println(a.getClass() == b.getClass());   // true - both ArrayList
```

When you need the type at runtime, pass a `Class<T>` token: `<T> T fromJson(String json, Class<T> type)` - this is how JSON libraries such as Jackson work.

## Common mistakes
- Using raw types (`List list`) and losing all compile-time checks.
- Expecting `List<Object>` to accept a `List<String>` argument - use `List<?>`.
- Trying to `add` to a `List<? extends Number>`.
- Writing `new T()` inside a generic class.

## Practice exercise
1. Write a generic class `Cache<K, V>` with `put`, `get` returning `Optional<V>`, and `size`.
2. Add a generic method `static <T extends Comparable<T>> List<T> topN(List<T> items, int n)` that returns the n largest items.
3. Write `static double average(Collection<? extends Number> values)` and call it with a `List<Integer>` and a `Set<Double>`.
4. Write `static void fillWithZeros(List<? super Integer> list, int count)`; call it with a `List<Number>`.
5. Explain in two sentences why `Cache<String, int>` does not compile.

## Key takeaways
- Generics move type errors from runtime to compile time and remove casts.
- Generic classes and interfaces declare type parameters; generic methods declare their own.
- Bounds (`T extends X`) let you call methods of the bound type.
- Wildcards make APIs flexible: Producer Extends, Consumer Super.
- Type erasure removes type arguments at runtime, which explains generics' restrictions.

## Learning objectives
- Create threads with Thread and Runnable and explain the thread lifecycle.
- Prevent race conditions and deadlocks with synchronization and safe designs.
- Use executors, thread pools, concurrent collections and CompletableFuture.

## Why multithreading
A **thread** is an independent path of execution inside a program. Every Java program starts with the `main` thread. Additional threads let a server handle many requests at once, keep a UI responsive while work runs in the background, and use all CPU cores. The price is complexity: threads share memory, so unsynchronised access to shared data produces bugs that appear only occasionally.

## Creating threads: Thread and Runnable
### Extending Thread
```java
class Downloader extends Thread {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Downloading on " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }
}
new Downloader().start();
```

### Implementing Runnable (preferred)
`Runnable` separates the *task* from the *thread*, and your class remains free to extend something else. Because `Runnable` has a single method, a lambda works.

```java
Runnable task = () -> System.out.println("Report on " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
Thread t = new Thread(task, "report-worker");
t.start();      // starts a new thread which calls run()
t.join();       // main waits for it to finish
```

Always call **`start()`**, not `run()`. Calling `run()` directly executes the code on the current thread - no new thread is created.

## Thread lifecycle
| State | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| NEW | Created, `start()` not yet called |
| RUNNABLE | Running or ready to run when the scheduler picks it |
| BLOCKED | Waiting to acquire a monitor lock |
| WAITING | Waiting indefinitely (`wait()`, `join()`) |
| TIMED_WAITING | Waiting with a timeout (`sleep(ms)`, `wait(ms)`, `join(ms)`) |
| TERMINATED | `run()` finished or threw an exception |

Useful methods: `Thread.sleep(ms)` pauses the current thread, `join()` waits for another thread, `interrupt()` politely asks a thread to stop (the thread must check `isInterrupted()` or handle `InterruptedException`), and `setDaemon(true)` marks a background thread that does not keep the JVM alive.

## Synchronization
When two threads perform a read-modify-write on the same variable, updates can be lost (a **race condition**).

```java
class Counter {
    private int count = 0;
    public void increment() { count++; }   // NOT atomic: read, add, write
    public int get() { return count; }
}

Counter c = new Counter();
Thread a = new Thread(() -> { for (int i = 0; i < 10_000; i++) c.increment(); });
Thread b = new Thread(() -> { for (int i = 0; i < 10_000; i++) c.increment(); });
a.start(); b.start(); a.join(); b.join();
System.out.println(c.get());   // often less than 20000!
```

Fixes:

```java
public synchronized void increment() { count++; }     // one thread at a time per object

// or a synchronized block on a lock object
private final Object lock = new Object();
public void increment() { synchronized (lock) { count++; } }

// or an atomic class - lock-free and simplest for counters
private final AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger();
public void increment() { count.incrementAndGet(); }
```

`synchronized` guarantees **mutual exclusion** and **visibility** (changes made inside are seen by the next thread that takes the lock). The `volatile` keyword guarantees visibility only - fine for a stop flag, not for `count++`. For advanced needs use `ReentrantLock` from `java.util.concurrent.locks` (supports `tryLock` with timeouts).

## Inter-thread communication
Threads can coordinate with `wait()`, `notify()` and `notifyAll()`, which must be called while holding the object's lock. `wait()` releases the lock and sleeps until notified.

```java
class Mailbox {
    private String message;
    public synchronized void put(String m) throws InterruptedException {
        while (message != null) wait();      // always wait in a loop
        message = m;
        notifyAll();
    }
    public synchronized String take() throws InterruptedException {
        while (message == null) wait();
        String m = message;
        message = null;
        notifyAll();
        return m;
    }
}
```

In real code, prefer a **`BlockingQueue`**, which implements this producer-consumer pattern for you:

```java
BlockingQueue<String> queue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(10);
new Thread(() -> { try { queue.put("order-1"); } catch (InterruptedException e) { } }).start();
System.out.println(queue.take());   // blocks until an item arrives: order-1
```

## Deadlock
A **deadlock** happens when threads wait for each other forever. Thread 1 holds lock A and waits for B; thread 2 holds B and waits for A.

```java
// Thread 1: synchronized(accountA) { synchronized(accountB) { transfer } }
// Thread 2: synchronized(accountB) { synchronized(accountA) { transfer } }   -> deadlock risk
```

Prevention:
- **Always acquire locks in the same global order** (for example by account ID).
- Hold locks for as short a time as possible and never call unknown code while holding one.
- Use `tryLock(timeout)` so a thread can back off.
- Use higher-level concurrency utilities instead of nested locks.

## Executor framework and thread pools
Creating a new thread per task is expensive and unbounded. An **ExecutorService** manages a **thread pool** and a task queue.

```java
ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);
List<Future<Integer>> results = new ArrayList<>();
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    int n = i;
    results.add(pool.submit(() -> n * n));      // Callable returns a value
}
for (Future<Integer> f : results) {
    System.out.print(f.get() + " ");            // 1 4 9 16 25
}
pool.shutdown();                                 // stop accepting tasks
pool.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
```

- `Runnable` returns nothing; `Callable<V>` returns a value and may throw a checked exception.
- `Future.get()` blocks until the result is ready; `get(timeout, unit)` avoids waiting forever.
- Pool types: `newFixedThreadPool(n)`, `newCachedThreadPool()`, `newSingleThreadExecutor()`, `newScheduledThreadPool(n)`, and since Java 21 `newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()` for lightweight virtual threads.
- Always `shutdown()` a pool you create, or use try-with-resources on Java 19+.

## Concurrent collections
Ordinary collections are not thread-safe. Use the `java.util.concurrent` versions:

| Collection | Use |
| --- | --- |
| `ConcurrentHashMap` | Thread-safe map with atomic `merge`, `compute`, `putIfAbsent` |
| `CopyOnWriteArrayList` | Lists that are read often and written rarely (listeners) |
| `BlockingQueue` (`LinkedBlockingQueue`, `ArrayBlockingQueue`) | Producer-consumer pipelines |
| `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` | Non-blocking queue |

```java
ConcurrentHashMap<String, Integer> hits = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
hits.merge("/home", 1, Integer::sum);   // atomic even with many threads
```

## CompletableFuture
`CompletableFuture` composes asynchronous steps without blocking, similar to JavaScript promises.

```java
CompletableFuture<String> user = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> "Asha");
CompletableFuture<Integer> orders = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> 3);

String summary = user
    .thenCombine(orders, (name, count) -> name + " has " + count + " orders")
    .exceptionally(ex -> "Could not load summary")
    .get();
System.out.println(summary);   // Asha has 3 orders
```

Key methods: `supplyAsync`/`runAsync` start work, `thenApply` transforms, `thenCompose` chains another async call, `thenCombine` joins two results, `allOf` waits for many, `exceptionally`/`handle` recover from errors, and `orTimeout` bounds waiting time.

## Common mistakes
- Calling `run()` instead of `start()`.
- Sharing a mutable `ArrayList` or `HashMap` across threads.
- Using `volatile` for compound operations like `count++`.
- Forgetting to shut down an executor, which keeps the JVM alive.
- Calling `wait()` outside a loop or outside `synchronized`.
- Testing concurrency by "it worked once" - races are timing-dependent.

## Practice exercise
1. Reproduce the lost-update counter bug with two threads, then fix it three ways: `synchronized`, `ReentrantLock` and `AtomicInteger`.
2. Build a producer-consumer pipeline with `BlockingQueue`: one thread reads order IDs, two worker threads "process" them.
3. Use a fixed thread pool to compute the sum of squares for 1..1000 in four chunks, collecting `Future` results.
4. Use `CompletableFuture` to load a user and their orders in parallel and combine the results, with a fallback on error.

## Key takeaways
- Prefer `Runnable`/`Callable` with an `ExecutorService` over raw threads.
- Shared mutable state needs synchronization, locks or atomic classes.
- Avoid deadlock by acquiring locks in a consistent order.
- Use concurrent collections and `BlockingQueue` for shared data.
- `CompletableFuture` composes asynchronous work with explicit error handling.

## Learning objectives
- Distinguish byte streams and character streams and when to use each.
- Read and write text files efficiently with buffered readers and writers.
- Handle files safely with try-with-resources, explicit encodings and path validation.

## Streams of bytes and characters
Java I/O is built on two families of classes:

| Family | Unit | Base classes | Use for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Byte streams | 8-bit bytes | `InputStream`, `OutputStream` | Images, PDFs, ZIP files, any binary data |
| Character streams | 16-bit chars | `Reader`, `Writer` | Text files (CSV, logs, JSON) |

Character streams convert bytes to characters using an **encoding**. Always use a known encoding such as UTF-8 so a file reads the same on every operating system.

All of these classes hold operating-system resources, so open them in **try-with-resources** (Module 3) to guarantee they are closed.

## The File class
`java.io.File` represents a path - it does not open or read anything.

```java
File file = new File("reports/summary.txt");
System.out.println(file.getName());          // summary.txt
System.out.println(file.exists());           // true or false
System.out.println(file.isDirectory());      // false
System.out.println(file.length());           // size in bytes (0 if missing)

File dir = new File("reports");
if (!dir.exists()) dir.mkdirs();              // create folders
String[] children = dir.list();               // names inside the folder
boolean deleted = file.delete();
```

The newer `java.nio.file` API (`Path`, `Files`) is more capable and reports clear exceptions; you will see both in real code.

```java
Path path = Path.of("reports", "summary.txt");
Files.createDirectories(path.getParent());
Files.writeString(path, "Hello", StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
String text = Files.readString(path, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
List<String> lines = Files.readAllLines(path);
```

## FileReader and FileWriter
`FileReader` and `FileWriter` read and write characters to a file.

```java
// Write (overwrites the file)
try (FileWriter writer = new FileWriter("notes.txt", StandardCharsets.UTF_8)) {
    writer.write("First line\n");
    writer.write("Second line\n");
}

// Append instead of overwrite: second argument true
try (FileWriter writer = new FileWriter("notes.txt", StandardCharsets.UTF_8, true)) {
    writer.write("Appended line\n");
}

// Read character by character
try (FileReader reader = new FileReader("notes.txt", StandardCharsets.UTF_8)) {
    int ch;
    while ((ch = reader.read()) != -1) {    // -1 means end of file
        System.out.print((char) ch);
    }
}
```

Reading one character at a time causes many small system calls. Wrap readers and writers in buffered versions.

## BufferedReader and BufferedWriter
Buffered classes read and write large chunks into memory, which is much faster, and add convenient methods: `readLine()` and `newLine()`.

```java
// Write a CSV report
try (BufferedWriter out = new BufferedWriter(new FileWriter("marks.csv", StandardCharsets.UTF_8))) {
    out.write("name,marks");
    out.newLine();
    out.write("Asha,88");
    out.newLine();
    out.write("Ravi,72");
    out.newLine();
}

// Read it line by line and compute the average
try (BufferedReader in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("marks.csv", StandardCharsets.UTF_8))) {
    String header = in.readLine();            // skip header
    String line;
    int total = 0, count = 0;
    while ((line = in.readLine()) != null) {  // null means end of file
        String[] parts = line.split(",");
        total += Integer.parseInt(parts[1].trim());
        count++;
    }
    System.out.println("Average: " + (double) total / count);   // Average: 80.0
}
```

A shortcut with the NIO API: `Files.newBufferedReader(path)` and `Files.lines(path)` (a stream of lines - close it with try-with-resources).

## InputStream and OutputStream
For binary files use byte streams. `FileInputStream` / `FileOutputStream` access files; `BufferedInputStream` / `BufferedOutputStream` add buffering.

```java
// Copy any file (image, PDF, ...) using a buffer
try (InputStream in = new BufferedInputStream(new FileInputStream("logo.png"));
     OutputStream out = new BufferedOutputStream(new FileOutputStream("logo-copy.png"))) {
    byte[] buffer = new byte[8192];
    int bytesRead;
    while ((bytesRead = in.read(buffer)) != -1) {
        out.write(buffer, 0, bytesRead);      // write only the bytes actually read
    }
}
// Modern one-liner: in.transferTo(out);  or  Files.copy(source, target)
```

Other useful byte streams: `DataInputStream`/`DataOutputStream` (read/write primitives), `ObjectInputStream`/`ObjectOutputStream` (Java serialization - avoid for untrusted data), and `ByteArrayInputStream` for in-memory data. In a web application, file uploads arrive as an `InputStream` (Module 28).

## Bridging bytes and characters
`InputStreamReader` and `OutputStreamWriter` convert between the two families with an explicit charset. This is how you read text from a network socket or an uploaded file:

```java
try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(
        new InputStreamReader(uploadedStream, StandardCharsets.UTF_8))) {
    reader.lines().forEach(System.out::println);
}
```

## Safety and good practice
- Always close streams - use try-with-resources.
- Specify `StandardCharsets.UTF_8`; relying on the platform default causes garbled text across systems.
- Handle `FileNotFoundException`/`NoSuchFileException` and `IOException` with useful messages.
- Treat file paths from users as untrusted: normalise them and check they stay inside an allowed folder, otherwise `../../etc/passwd` style input can escape (path traversal).
- Write to a temporary file and then move it into place when a half-written file would be harmful.
- Do not load huge files fully into memory; stream them line by line.

## Common mistakes
- Forgetting to close a writer, so buffered data never reaches the disk.
- Using `FileWriter` without the append flag and accidentally overwriting a file.
- Writing `out.write(buffer)` instead of `out.write(buffer, 0, bytesRead)`, which copies stale bytes at the end.
- Reading binary files with a `Reader`, which corrupts them.

## Practice exercise
1. Write `ExpenseLog` that appends `date,category,amount` lines to `expenses.csv` using `BufferedWriter` in append mode.
2. Read the file with `BufferedReader` and print totals per category using a `Map<String, Double>`.
3. Skip and report malformed lines (wrong column count or non-numeric amount) with their line numbers.
4. Copy `expenses.csv` to `backup/expenses-<today>.csv` using byte streams and a buffer, creating the folder if needed.

## Key takeaways
- Byte streams handle binary data; character streams handle text with an encoding.
- `File`/`Path` describe locations; streams, readers and writers move data.
- Buffered classes are faster and provide `readLine`/`newLine`.
- Use try-with-resources and explicit UTF-8 every time.
- Validate user-supplied paths and stream large files instead of loading them whole.

## Learning objectives
- Connect Java to a relational database with a JDBC driver and URL.
- Execute safe queries with PreparedStatement and process ResultSets.
- Manage transactions, batching, RowSets and connection pooling.

## JDBC introduction
**JDBC (Java Database Connectivity)** is the standard Java API for talking to relational databases. Your code uses JDBC interfaces from `java.sql`; a database-specific **driver** implements them. Swap the driver and the same code can talk to MySQL, PostgreSQL or Oracle.

```
Java application → JDBC API (java.sql) → JDBC driver (e.g. MySQL Connector/J) → Database
```

Spring's `JdbcTemplate`, JPA and Hibernate are all built on JDBC, so understanding it explains what those frameworks do for you.

Add the MySQL driver with Gradle:

```groovy
dependencies {
    implementation 'com.mysql:mysql-connector-j:8.4.0'
}
```

## Driver types
| Type | Name | How it works | Today |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Type 1 | JDBC-ODBC bridge | Translates JDBC calls to ODBC | Removed in Java 8 |
| Type 2 | Native-API | Calls the database's native client library | Rare |
| Type 3 | Network protocol | Talks to a middleware server that talks to the DB | Rare |
| Type 4 | Thin / pure Java | Speaks the database's network protocol directly | **Standard choice** (MySQL Connector/J, PostgreSQL driver) |

Modern drivers register themselves automatically (JDBC 4+), so `Class.forName("com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver")` is no longer required.

## Establishing a connection
A `Connection` represents a session with the database. `DriverManager.getConnection` opens one from a **JDBC URL**:

```java
import java.sql.*;

public class ConnectDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/training_db";
        String user = System.getenv("DB_USER");          // keep credentials out of code
        String password = System.getenv("DB_PASSWORD");

        try (Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, password)) {
            System.out.println("Connected: " + conn.getMetaData().getDatabaseProductName());
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            System.out.println("Connection failed: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

Steps of every JDBC program:
1. Get a `Connection`.
2. Create a `Statement`, `PreparedStatement` or `CallableStatement`.
3. Execute: `executeQuery` (SELECT → `ResultSet`), `executeUpdate` (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE → row count) or `execute`.
4. Process the `ResultSet`.
5. Close everything - use **try-with-resources**.

## Statement, PreparedStatement and CallableStatement
### Statement
For fixed SQL with no user input:

```java
try (Statement st = conn.createStatement();
     ResultSet rs = st.executeQuery("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM course")) {
    rs.next();
    System.out.println("Courses: " + rs.getInt(1));
}
```

### The SQL injection problem
Never concatenate user input into SQL:

```java
// DANGEROUS
String sql = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '" + email + "'";
// email = "' OR '1'='1"  →  returns every user
```

### PreparedStatement (use this)
`?` placeholders are sent separately from the values, so input can never change the SQL structure. The statement is also precompiled and reusable.

```java
String sql = "SELECT id, title, fee FROM course WHERE level = ? AND fee <= ?";
try (PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {
    ps.setString(1, "BEGINNER");     // parameter indexes start at 1
    ps.setBigDecimal(2, new BigDecimal("6000"));
    try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {
        while (rs.next()) {
            System.out.printf("%d %s %s%n", rs.getInt("id"), rs.getString("title"), rs.getBigDecimal("fee"));
        }
    }
}

// Insert and read the generated key
String insert = "INSERT INTO course(code, title, fee) VALUES (?, ?, ?)";
try (PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(insert, Statement.RETURN_GENERATED_KEYS)) {
    ps.setString(1, "SQL101");
    ps.setString(2, "SQL Basics");
    ps.setBigDecimal(3, new BigDecimal("2999"));
    int rows = ps.executeUpdate();                // 1
    try (ResultSet keys = ps.getGeneratedKeys()) {
        if (keys.next()) System.out.println("New id: " + keys.getLong(1));
    }
}
```

Placeholders work for values only - not for table or column names. If those must vary, choose from a fixed allow-list.

### CallableStatement
Calls stored procedures:

```java
try (CallableStatement cs = conn.prepareCall("{call give_raise(?, ?)}")) {
    cs.setInt(1, 1);
    cs.setBigDecimal(2, new BigDecimal("5"));
    cs.execute();
}
```

OUT parameters are registered with `cs.registerOutParameter(index, Types.INTEGER)` and read after execution.

## ResultSet
A `ResultSet` is a cursor over query rows; it starts **before** the first row, so call `next()` first.

```java
List<Course> courses = new ArrayList<>();
try (PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement("SELECT id, code, title FROM course ORDER BY title");
     ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {
    while (rs.next()) {
        courses.add(new Course(rs.getLong("id"), rs.getString("code"), rs.getString("title")));
    }
}
```

- Read by column label (`rs.getString("title")`) or 1-based index.
- `getInt` returns `0` for SQL NULL; call `rs.wasNull()` or use `rs.getObject("col", Integer.class)`.
- Default result sets are forward-only and read-only; scrollable/updatable ones are requested with `createStatement(ResultSet.TYPE_SCROLL_INSENSITIVE, ResultSet.CONCUR_READ_ONLY)`.
- `ResultSetMetaData` describes column names and types.

## Transactions
By default each statement auto-commits. To make several changes atomic, turn auto-commit off:

```java
public void transfer(Connection conn, long from, long to, BigDecimal amount) throws SQLException {
    String debit = "UPDATE account SET balance = balance - ? WHERE id = ? AND balance >= ?";
    String credit = "UPDATE account SET balance = balance + ? WHERE id = ?";
    boolean oldAutoCommit = conn.getAutoCommit();
    conn.setAutoCommit(false);
    try (PreparedStatement d = conn.prepareStatement(debit);
         PreparedStatement c = conn.prepareStatement(credit)) {
        d.setBigDecimal(1, amount); d.setLong(2, from); d.setBigDecimal(3, amount);
        if (d.executeUpdate() != 1) throw new SQLException("Insufficient funds or missing account");
        c.setBigDecimal(1, amount); c.setLong(2, to);
        if (c.executeUpdate() != 1) throw new SQLException("Target account missing");
        conn.commit();
    } catch (SQLException e) {
        conn.rollback();          // undo the debit too
        throw e;
    } finally {
        conn.setAutoCommit(oldAutoCommit);   // restore state (important with pools)
    }
}
```

Use `conn.setSavepoint()` for partial rollbacks and `conn.setTransactionIsolation(...)` to choose an isolation level.

## Batch processing
Sending many statements one by one costs a network round trip each. **Batching** groups them:

```java
String sql = "INSERT INTO enrollment(employee_id, course_id) VALUES (?, ?)";
conn.setAutoCommit(false);
try (PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {
    for (long employeeId : employeeIds) {
        ps.setLong(1, employeeId);
        ps.setLong(2, courseId);
        ps.addBatch();
    }
    int[] counts = ps.executeBatch();
    conn.commit();
    System.out.println("Inserted " + counts.length + " rows");
}
```

For MySQL, add `rewriteBatchedStatements=true` to the JDBC URL for a large speed-up. For very large imports, execute the batch every 500-1000 rows.

## RowSet
A **RowSet** (`javax.sql.rowset`) is a `ResultSet` that is also a JavaBean and can be configured with properties.
- `JdbcRowSet` - connected; a scrollable, updatable wrapper around a result set.
- `CachedRowSet` - **disconnected**; loads rows into memory, closes the connection, can be modified offline and synced back.
- `WebRowSet`, `FilteredRowSet`, `JoinRowSet` - add XML export, filtering and joins.

```java
CachedRowSet rs = RowSetProvider.newFactory().createCachedRowSet();
rs.setUrl(url); rs.setUsername(user); rs.setPassword(password);
rs.setCommand("SELECT code, title FROM course");
rs.execute();                 // connection is closed after loading
while (rs.next()) System.out.println(rs.getString("code"));
```

RowSets are rarely used in new Spring applications, but they appear in older enterprise code.

## Connection pooling
Opening a database connection is slow (network handshake, authentication). A **connection pool** keeps a set of open connections and lends them out. Calling `close()` on a pooled connection returns it to the pool instead of closing it.

```java
HikariConfig config = new HikariConfig();
config.setJdbcUrl("jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/training_db");
config.setUsername(System.getenv("DB_USER"));
config.setPassword(System.getenv("DB_PASSWORD"));
config.setMaximumPoolSize(10);
config.setConnectionTimeout(3000);           // ms to wait for a free connection

try (HikariDataSource ds = new HikariDataSource(config);
     Connection conn = ds.getConnection()) {   // borrowed from the pool
    // use conn
}                                               // returned to the pool
```

**HikariCP** is the default pool in Spring Boot - you just set `spring.datasource.*` properties. Size pools modestly: more connections than the database can serve makes things slower. Always restore connection state (auto-commit, isolation) before returning a connection.

## Common mistakes
- Building SQL with string concatenation (SQL injection).
- Not closing `ResultSet`/`Statement`/`Connection`, leaking resources until the pool is exhausted.
- Forgetting `rs.next()` before reading.
- Leaving auto-commit off or a transaction open after an error.
- Hard-coding passwords in source code.

## Practice exercise
Build a console `CourseDao` for the MySQL `training_db`:
1. `save(Course)` returning the generated id, `findById(long)` returning `Optional<Course>`, `findByLevel(String)`, `updateFee(long, BigDecimal)` and `delete(long)` - all with `PreparedStatement`.
2. `enrollAll(long courseId, List<Long> employeeIds)` using batching inside one transaction.
3. A `transferEnrollment` method that removes one enrollment and adds another atomically; prove rollback by forcing the second step to fail.
4. Replace `DriverManager` with a HikariCP `DataSource`.

## Key takeaways
- JDBC = standard interfaces + vendor driver; Type 4 thin drivers are the norm.
- Always use `PreparedStatement` for input values - it prevents SQL injection.
- `ResultSet` starts before the first row; close all JDBC objects with try-with-resources.
- Disable auto-commit and commit/rollback to make multi-statement work atomic.
- Use batching for bulk writes and a connection pool (HikariCP) in real applications.

## Learning objectives
- Apply selectors, the cascade and specificity to style elements predictably.
- Control spacing and layout with the box model, display, position, Flexbox and Grid.
- Build responsive, themeable designs with media queries, variables, transitions and animations.

## CSS introduction
**CSS (Cascading Style Sheets)** controls how HTML looks: colours, fonts, spacing and layout. A CSS **rule** has a selector and declarations:

```css
h1 {                 /* selector */
  color: #1d4ed8;    /* property: value; */
  font-size: 2rem;
}
```

Three ways to add CSS:
- **External stylesheet** (best): `<link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">`
- **Internal**: a `<style>` block in `<head>`
- **Inline**: `<p style="color:red">` (hard to maintain; avoid)

"Cascading" means several rules can apply to one element; the browser decides by **importance** (`!important`), **specificity** and **source order** (later wins). Some properties, such as `color` and `font-family`, are **inherited** by child elements.

## Selectors
| Selector | Example | Selects |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Type | `p` | All `<p>` elements |
| Class | `.card` | Elements with `class="card"` |
| ID | `#main` | The element with `id="main"` |
| Universal | `*` | Everything |
| Descendant | `nav a` | `<a>` anywhere inside `<nav>` |
| Child | `ul > li` | Direct `<li>` children |
| Adjacent / general sibling | `h2 + p`, `h2 ~ p` | Next / following siblings |
| Attribute | `input[type="email"]` | Matching attribute |
| Pseudo-class | `a:hover`, `li:first-child`, `input:focus`, `:not(.done)` | Element state or position |
| Pseudo-element | `p::first-line`, `.req::after` | Part of an element / generated content |
| Group | `h1, h2, h3` | Several selectors |

**Specificity** (highest wins): inline styles > IDs > classes/attributes/pseudo-classes > element types. Prefer classes for styling; IDs make rules hard to override.

## Colors, fonts and backgrounds
```css
:root { color-scheme: light; }
body {
  font-family: "Inter", system-ui, sans-serif;   /* fallbacks */
  font-size: 16px;
  line-height: 1.6;
  color: #1f2937;
  background-color: #f9fafb;
}
.title { font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.5rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .05em; }
.hero {
  background-image: linear-gradient(135deg, #1d4ed8, #7c3aed);
  background-size: cover;
  background-position: center;
  color: white;
}
```

- Colours: names (`red`), hex (`#1d4ed8`), `rgb(29 78 216)`, `rgb(0 0 0 / 50%)` with transparency, `hsl(220 80% 50%)`.
- Ensure enough **contrast** between text and background (WCAG recommends at least 4.5:1 for body text).
- Units: `px` (fixed), `rem` (relative to root font size - good for accessibility), `em` (relative to the parent), `%`, `vw`/`vh` (viewport).
- Web fonts load with `@font-face` or a Google Fonts `<link>`.

## Box model: margin, border, padding
Every element is a rectangular box: **content → padding → border → margin** (from inside out).

```css
* { box-sizing: border-box; }          /* width includes padding and border */
.card {
  width: 300px;
  padding: 16px;                       /* space inside the border */
  border: 1px solid #e5e7eb;
  border-radius: 12px;                 /* rounded corners */
  margin: 16px auto;                   /* space outside; auto centres horizontally */
}
```

With the default `content-box`, the card above would be 300 + 32 + 2 = 334px wide; with `border-box` it is exactly 300px - which is why most projects set it globally. Shorthand order is **top, right, bottom, left** (clockwise): `margin: 10px 20px` means 10px top/bottom and 20px left/right. Vertical margins between blocks can **collapse** into the larger one.

## Display and position
### display
| Value | Behaviour |
| --- | --- |
| `block` | New line, full width, width/height apply |
| `inline` | Flows in text; width/height ignored |
| `inline-block` | Flows in text but respects width/height |
| `none` | Removed from layout (not rendered) |
| `flex` / `grid` | Turns the element into a flex or grid container |

`visibility: hidden` hides an element but keeps its space.

### position
| Value | Behaviour |
| --- | --- |
| `static` | Default; normal flow |
| `relative` | Offset from its normal place; becomes a reference for absolute children |
| `absolute` | Removed from flow; placed relative to the nearest positioned ancestor |
| `fixed` | Placed relative to the viewport; stays when scrolling |
| `sticky` | Normal until a scroll threshold, then sticks |

```css
.badge-wrapper { position: relative; }
.badge { position: absolute; top: 8px; right: 8px; }
.site-header { position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 10; }
```

`z-index` controls stacking order for positioned elements.

## Flexbox
**Flexbox** lays items out in one dimension - a row or a column.

```css
.toolbar {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: row;          /* or column */
  justify-content: space-between;   /* main axis: start | center | end | space-between | space-around */
  align-items: center;          /* cross axis */
  gap: 12px;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}
.toolbar .search { flex: 1; }   /* grow to fill remaining space */
```

Perfect centring: `display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center;`. Use flexbox for navbars, toolbars, card footers and aligning items in a row.

## Grid
**CSS Grid** lays items out in two dimensions - rows and columns.

```css
.course-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr));   /* responsive with no media query */
  gap: 20px;
}

.page {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 240px 1fr;
  grid-template-areas:
    "header header"
    "sidebar main"
    "footer footer";
}
.page header { grid-area: header; }
.page aside  { grid-area: sidebar; }
```

`fr` is a fraction of the free space. Rule of thumb: **Grid for page layout, Flexbox for components**.

## Media queries and responsive design
**Responsive design** makes one page work on phones, tablets and desktops.

```css
/* Mobile-first: base styles target small screens */
.course-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 16px; }

@media (min-width: 768px) {            /* tablets and up */
  .course-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
}
@media (min-width: 1200px) {           /* desktops */
  .course-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
}
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  body { background: #111827; color: #f9fafb; }
}
img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }   /* images never overflow */
```

Principles: include the viewport meta tag, design **mobile-first**, use fluid units (`%`, `fr`, `rem`, `clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.5rem)`), avoid fixed widths and test with browser DevTools device mode.

## CSS variables
**Custom properties** store reusable values and can change at runtime (for theming).

```css
:root {
  --brand: #1d4ed8;
  --radius: 12px;
  --space: 16px;
}
.button { background: var(--brand); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: calc(var(--space) / 2) var(--space); }
[data-theme="dark"] { --brand: #60a5fa; }
```

## Transitions and animations
**Transitions** animate a property change between two states:

```css
.button { transition: background-color .2s ease, transform .2s ease; }
.button:hover { background-color: #1e40af; transform: translateY(-2px); }
```

**Animations** use `@keyframes` for multi-step motion:

```css
@keyframes spin { from { transform: rotate(0); } to { transform: rotate(360deg); } }
.loader { animation: spin 1s linear infinite; }

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  * { animation: none !important; transition: none !important; }
}
```

Animate `transform` and `opacity` for smooth performance, and respect users who prefer reduced motion.

## Common mistakes
- Fixed pixel widths that overflow on phones.
- Overusing `!important` and IDs, causing specificity wars.
- Forgetting `box-sizing: border-box`.
- Low-contrast text and removing focus outlines without a visible replacement.
- Using absolute positioning for whole layouts instead of flex/grid.

